import com.sun.management.ThreadMXBean
import java.lang.management.ManagementFactory

private val THREADS = ManagementFactory.getThreadMXBean() as ThreadMXBean

@Volatile
private var sink: Any? = null                                     // results escape here, so nothing is optimized away

private fun bytes(work: () -> Unit): Long {
    val before = THREADS.currentThreadAllocatedBytes
    work()
    return THREADS.currentThreadAllocatedBytes - before
}

private fun show(what: String, work: () -> Unit) {
    val first = bytes(work)                                        // the first run also pays for one-time setup
    val second = bytes(work)
    println("%-34s %,10d %,11d".format(what, first, second))
}

/** Counts the bytes a few small pieces of code allocate, twice each, using the JVM's per-thread counter. */
fun main() {
    println("%-34s %10s %11s".format("bytes allocated by", "first run", "second run"))
    show("ByteArray(1 MiB)") { sink = ByteArray(1 shl 20) }
    show("1,000 x boxing (i % 128).toLong()") { for (i in 0 until 1_000) sink = (i % 128).toLong() }
    show("1,000 x boxing (1_000 + i).toLong()") { for (i in 0 until 1_000) sink = (1_000 + i).toLong() }
    show("1,000 x \"img-\$i\"") { for (i in 0 until 1_000) sink = "img-$i" }

    Thread.ofVirtual().start {
        println("on a virtual thread the counter says ${THREADS.currentThreadAllocatedBytes}")
    }.join()
}

sections.body:
C2 is the JIT compiler that optimizes a program's hottest methods, and it pays close attention. First it does **inlining**: it copies the small methods a method calls into the method's own body. Then it runs **escape analysis**, which checks whether an object can be seen anywhere outside the method it's compiling. If it can't, C2 can skip the object and keep its fields in registers, the processor's own small storage slots. That step is called **scalar replacement**. In the warehouse, it's a worker noticing that a box never leaves the desk, and writing its contents on the notepad instead.

Here's a loop that makes a million short-lived points:

```java
record Point(long x, long y) {}

static long sumPoints(int n) {
    long s = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        Point p = new Point(i, i + 1);   // p never leaves the method
        s += p.x() + p.y();
    }
    return s;
}
```

```kotlin
data class Point(val x: Long, val y: Long)

fun sumPoints(n: Int): Long {
    var s = 0L
    for (i in 0 until n) {
        val p = Point(i.toLong(), i + 1L)   // p never leaves the function
        s += p.x + p.y
    }
    return s
}
```

A `Point` is a 12-byte header plus two `long`s, 28 bytes, padded to 32. So a call with n = 1,000,000 should allocate 32,000,000 bytes. Call it ten times and count each call on JDK 25:

```text
round 0:    3,282,776 bytes
round 1:    1,572,832 bytes
round 2:      262,112 bytes
round 3:            0 bytes
...
round 9:            0 bytes
```

Same code, same `new`, zero bytes. With `-XX:-DoEscapeAnalysis`, which switches the analysis off, every round allocated 32,000,000 bytes: a million 32-byte points.

Now look at the first rounds. The interpreter, and C1 (the quick JIT compiler that runs first), create every object. The allocation only disappears once C2 has compiled the method, and when that happens depends on timing: one run of the Kotlin twin on JDK 21 reached 0 only at round 5. Here the warehouse picture breaks again. A person would notice the first time that a box never leaves the desk. C2 notices only after it has watched a method run thousands of times, and only inside the code it inlined.

**Escape analysis is a bonus, not a promise. Never assert "allocates nothing" in a unit test.** It can pass on your laptop and fail on a busier machine. The lab asserts only the allocations the code really needs.

An object escapes when you store it in a field, an array or a collection, return it, or pass it to a method C2 didn't inline. C2 also won't scalar-replace an array with more than 64 elements (the `EliminateAllocationArraySizeLimit` flag). So escape analysis helps tight loops. It can't help a design that keeps every size in a `HashMap<Integer, Long>`, because those boxes live on inside the map. And that's where most of a program's bytes come from.

